  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 359 AM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nToday is the day of the monsoon's return! However, that does not \nautomatically mean that every part of our CWA will receive moisture \nequally, so let's break things down. From a large-scale \nperspective, the upper level ridge axis mentioned in previous AFDs\nis finally shifting eastwards over the central U.S., which favors\nPacific moisture transport due to southwest flow aloft. This \nchange in the synoptic pattern alone will promote precipitable \nwater (PW) values that greatly exceed the climatological average \nover the entire state of Colorado, starting today and persisting \nthrough at least Saturday. As such, temperatures will also trend \nmuch cooler, with this afternoon being the last day we have \n90-degree highs in the forecast until Tuesday. \n\nRain coverage will begin to increase over the mountains first early \nthis afternoon as moisture flows in from west to east, and as the \nday's heating destabilizes the high terrain. Residual dry air near \nthe surface may diminish the efficiency of rainfall production for \nthe Denver metro and I-25 corridor, at least early in the afternoon. \nHigh-res model guidance does suggest a second round of showers \nmoving off the foothills early in the evening , which would provide \na better chance of more scattered rainfall coverage over this \narea.\n\nThe northeast Colorado plains will have the best chance of seeing \nheavier rainfall rates today due to the chance for a few strong \nthunderstorms late in the afternoon. The SPC has placed our \nnortheastermost counties in a Slight (2/5) risk for severe \nthunderstorms, with damaging winds and large hail being the primary \nhazards. Higher dewpoints being transported by southeasterly \nsurface winds, around 30KT of 0-6km shear, steep mid-level lapse\nrates (~8 C/km), and MLCAPE >2,000 J/kg would definitely support \nthe development of a few strong thunderstorms over this area. \nAdditionally, recent satellite imagery and station plots show an \noutflow boundary that originated from last night's convection over\nNebraska heading towards northeast Colorado. Behind this outflow \nboundary are 60-degree dewpoints, which could provide an \nadditional boost for thunderstorm development in this area.\n\nThe forecast remains on track for Thursday afternoon/evening being \nthe peak of moisture transport for this week's monsoonal event. \nRainfall production will also be most efficient on this day, with \nwarm cloud layer depths reaching (and at times exceeding) 5,000 \nft, and storm motion vectors ~15KT. Because of these parameters, \nthe anomalous moisture already in place, and soils likely being \nprimed from the previous day's rain, Thursday appears to have the \nhighest potential for flash flooding. It remains difficult to tell\nexactly where the strongest showers and thunderstorms will occur.\nOn one hand, the mountains require less forcing and instability \ndue to orographic enhancement. On the other hand, east to \nsoutheasterly surface winds may bring more plentiful moisture onto\nthe eastern Colorado plains, where better wind shear may support \nstronger thunderstorms with high rainfall rates. \n\nFriday does not look too different from Thursday, with showers and \nthunderstorms becoming numerous in the early afternoon, from the \nhigh terrain to the low elevations. Ensemble model guidance suggests \na slight decrease in available moisture and instability for Saturday \nand Sunday, although both days will still have the necessary \ningredients to produce scattered afternoon showers and storms,\ngiven that the current pattern does not change until Monday or \nTuesday. Even then, long-range ensembles continue to suggest \nabove-average PWs and an active monsoon through at least the end \nof next week.",
